### What do you want to change?

AI-assisted report; the synthetic reproduction below was verified locally.

Make wrapped authentication failures non-retryable in `isRetryableAssistantError`. I'd like to implement this fix and its regression tests; may I submit a PR (`lgtm` approval under the contribution guide)?

### Why?

On Pi 1.0.4 and 1.2.0, adding a gateway prefix changes an invalid-key error from permanent to retryable. An embedded AgentSession with three retries then makes four requests before reporting authentication failure.

This minimal reproduction calls only pi-ai; no session, extensions, credentials or network are involved:

```js
import { fauxAssistantMessage, isRetryableAssistantError } from '@earendil-works/pi-ai';

for (const text of ['401 invalid api key', 'Provider returned error: 401 invalid api key']) {
  const message = fauxAssistantMessage('', { stopReason: 'error', errorMessage: text });
  console.log(isRetryableAssistantError(message));
}
```

Actual: `false`, `true`. Expected: `false`, `false`. The generic `provider.?returned.?error` pattern overrides the authentication meaning, wasting requests and delaying an actionable error.

### How? (optional)

Reuse the existing classifier and reject explicit authentication failures before generic transient/wrapper matching. Add regression cases for direct/wrapped 401 errors and controls preserving 429/503 retries. Keep provider OAuth refresh, retry budgets, delay policy and extension APIs unchanged. I will wait for maintainer approval before opening a PR.

**Bug report: `acquireTrustLockSync` busy-waits on `Date.now()` — a worker spins at 100% CPU forever when vitest fake timers are active**

## Symptom

`vitest run` hangs indefinitely; one worker process (`dist/workers/forks.js`) spins at ~100% CPU forever. Occurred repeatedly in a large extension test suite (orphaned workers kept spinning for 15+ minutes; sampled via CDP inspector 12 times — every sample had `acquireTrustLockSync` as the top frame, and `Date.now()` evaluated twice 1.5s apart returned the identical value, confirming a frozen clock).

## Root cause

`packages/coding-agent/src/core/trust-manager.ts` (`acquireTrustLockSync`, also bundled in `dist/bundle/chunks/chunk-GXLAGZ3Z.js`), present in 1.1.0, 1.2.0, and current `main`:

```ts
const maxAttempts = 10, delayMs = 20;
for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= maxAttempts; attempt++) {
  try { return lockfile.lockSync(trustDir, { realpath: false, lockfilePath: `${path}.lock` }); }
  catch (error) {
    if (code !== "ELOCKED" || attempt === maxAttempts) throw error;
    const start = Date.now();
    while (Date.now() - start < delayMs) { /* spin */ }  // ← never exits if Date.now() is frozen
  }
}
```

The retry backoff is a synchronous busy-wait on the wall clock. The designed worst case is 10 × 20ms ≈ 200ms, then throw.

## Trigger chain

1. A test enables vitest fake timers → `Date.now()` is mocked/frozen in that worker.
2. Module-init code hits the trust read — e.g. `seedProjectTrust(cwd)` → `ProjectTrustStore.get()` → `withTrustFileLock` — and gets ELOCKED. Two ways this happens in practice:
   - a stale `trust.json.lock` left by a previously killed run (note: `lockfile.lockSync` here passes no `stale` option, so an orphaned lock is never reclaimed — related to #4919, which added `stale` to other sync locks);
   - ordinary cross-worker contention: another test worker holds the lock for its own trust read.
3. `Date.now() - start < delayMs` is eternally true → infinite 100%-CPU spin. The designed give-up-then-throw is never reached, so callers' `catch` (e.g. `seedProjectTrust`'s) never fires.

Note the frozen clock makes this worse than ordinary contention: the spin never re-checks the lock, so the worker hangs **even after the peer releases it**.

## Suggested fix

Replace the clock-based spin with a real blocking sleep that timer mocks cannot intercept:

```ts
const sleepSync = (ms: number) => {
  Atomics.wait(new Int32Array(new SharedArrayBuffer(4)), 0, 0, ms);
};
// in the catch: sleepSync(delayMs);
```

`Atomics.wait` blocks on the futex using real time. Alternative: drop the sleep entirely and retry immediately (the lock hold times here are microseconds).

## Reproduction sketch

1. In a vitest worker: `vi.useFakeTimers()` (freezes `Date.now()`).
2. Create `trust.json.lock` (or hold it from another process).
3. Trigger a trust read (e.g. `new ProjectTrustStore(agentDir).get(cwd)`).
4. Worker spins at 100% CPU forever; no timeout, no SIGINT response from that worker.

## Related

- #3458 — same failure mode (`acquireLockSyncWithRetry` in `settings-manager.ts`): "vitest concurrently … randomly stuck on deadlock", one core pinned, no progress. Closed as no-action. That report attributed the livelock to a blocked peer `release()`; this one is distinct — a frozen `Date.now()` prevents termination even with no peer.
- #4919 — sync locks lacked `stale`; the trust-manager `lockSync` call still passes no `stale`, which is why stale ELOCKEDs occur at all.
- #8927 — adjacent auth-store sync path with the same 10 × 20ms busy-wait pattern.

## Environment

- `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent` 1.1.0 (verified byte-identical in 1.2.0 and on `main` — `trust-manager.ts` last touched by 8562bcf6, 2026-09-29)
- Node 25.8.2, vitest 4.1.11, Linux

### What happened?

Since there's no UI to configure OpenRouter providers, I configured `models.json` with an override:

```json
{
  "providers": {
    "openrouter": {
      "apiKey": "sk-or-v1-63139...",
      "modelOverrides": {
        "minimax/minimax-m3": {
          "name": "MiniMax M3 [rasmus@openrouter, parasail]",
          "compat": {
            "openRouterRouting": {
              "only": ["parasail/fp8"],
              "allow_fallbacks": false
            }
          }
        },
        "deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash": {
          "name": "DeepSeek V4 Flash [rasmus@openrouter, baidu|gmicloud]",
          "compat": {
            "openRouterRouting": {
              "only": ["baidu/fp8", "gmicloud/fp8"],
              "allow_fallbacks": false
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

I just want to choose my providers on OpenRouter - and I want "proof" that it's working, so I'm overriding the name.

Unfortunately, this has no effect on search - so you can see:

<img width="1376" height="576" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c055d99e-676b-4a18-9b40-a7157f54781f" />

It *does* have the name override, so at least I have proof the config is working.

However:

<img width="1376" height="708" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/147a9645-f6ee-4e33-a773-48ba4d40ac39" />

It does not seem to query the name? I can't find or keep track of my overrides.

I don't even know why any results come up at all? surely none of these have "rasmus" in the name. 😅

Could we either:

1. just make the search work for names (why doesn't it?)
  1.b. display the names on the list (why do we have names that aren't displayed?)
2. allow an `id` override, so we can change the ID that's actually visible on the list

I guess, ideally, I think the built-in providers should actually provide configuration UI? Since apparently it is very difficult to find documentation for the right keys/values to put in openrouter configuration.

I also tried adding a new openrouter provider, but neither Pi or I could find any way to make it respect the thinking option in Pi - it even plowed through the source code, and eventually concluded it couldn't make this work reliable with a custom provider.

So one other approach to consider: what if we could *extend* existing providers? Like an `extends` key for custom providers - which would make it work just like `modelOverrides`, but effectively creating a new provider in the listing rather than overriding. This would solve the search problem, and at the same time would give you easier and more reliable custom providers without needing to figure out how to replicate what Pi does internally.

It would probably fit the use case of "I just want to specify allowed providers on OpenRouter" better? Or really any scenario where you want to configure something *without* replacing the built-in options.


