#### Summary

| Question | Result |
|---|---|
| Does the real `memory rem-harness` CLI (REM preview, which calls `dedupeEntries`) produce the same output on base and head? | Yes. 8 runs per build over fresh copies of the same populated state: identical `rem` section hash, identical normalized deep section hash, identical deep order hash. |
| Does the real gateway light sweep (managed dreaming cron job, `prepareLightDreaming`: dedupe plus diary-coverage ordering) produce the same output? | Same candidate set and same sort-key sequence in 34 of 34 diary runs and 6 of 6 no-diary runs. Exact order differs from run to run on exact-tie entries (11 of 34 diary runs), on BOTH builds (base 4 of 17, head 7 of 17). See "Tie-order variation". |
| Does diary coverage visibly matter in the real sweep? | Yes. With 4 synthetic diary pages in `DREAMS.md`, 9 covered candidates leave the top 100 and 9 uncovered ones enter, identically on base and head. |
| Speed on the real seam | `memory rem-harness` wall 2.01, 2.00, 1.94 s on base vs 1.87, 1.87, 1.81 s on head (about 0.13 s, 6 to 7 percent, of a roughly 2 s CLI run). `--cpu-prof`: self time in `tokenize` 80.3 ms on base, not sampled on head. |
| Defect or behavioral difference attributable to the PR | None found. |
| Honest limit | The production short-term recall store is capped at 512 entries (`SHORT_TERM_RECALL_MAX_ENTRIES = 512`, `short-term-promotion-utils.ts:28`), so the real seam never reaches the 2,000-entry size of the benchmark above. At 512 entries the saving is tens of milliseconds, not hundreds. The end-to-end gateway sweep is about 8.1 s on both builds and is dominated by other work, so no end-to-end speedup is claimed. |

#### What is synthetic

- All memory text is generated by `gen-ws.mjs` (seeded, homelab-style notes, no private data): 60 daily files `memory/2026-07-31.md` to `memory/2026-09-28.md`, 1,246,416 bytes, plus a `MEMORY.md`. 20 big files (about 57 KB each) hold repeated line families so many chunks are near-duplicates within a path.
- Embeddings are a local deterministic stub (`stub-embed.mjs`, 127.0.0.1:18921, sha256-derived 64-dim vectors). No real model or network service was used for embeddings.
- The recall store was populated by production code only (no hand edits): `memory index --force`, then 180 real `memory search` CLI calls.
- The 4 diary pages in `DREAMS.md` are synthetic: each quotes the first 300 characters of one light candidate from the no-diary run (ranks 1, 3, 5, 7) so they overlap recall snippets. Not production-written.
- The dreaming narrative (LLM) step was not run for real: the isolated config points the `openai` provider `baseUrl` at the local stub, which answers 404, so narratives fall back to the local diary fallback. The light and REM reports are written before and independent of the narrative.
- Workspace was built so no daily file falls inside the 2 day light ingestion window, to keep the sweep input exactly the 512 recalled entries (see Harness notes).

