Closes #136262

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes an issue where users of OpenAI-compatible providers would see live assistant text double (n → 2n) mid-message when the provider intermittently resends the entire accumulated text as a single bare `text_delta` frame. Because downstream consumers append text deltas additively, the replayed full content is appended onto the text that is already there; the next provider snapshot corrects it back, producing a visible n → 2n → n oscillation in live channels and dashboards until the message completes.

## Why This Change Was Made

The `AssistantMessageEvent` contract defines `text_delta` as additive: consumers that need the current text rebuild it by replaying deltas from the latest start/end partial checkpoint. A provider frame whose payload exactly equals the whole accumulated visible text violates that producer-side invariant — it is a cumulative snapshot wearing a delta's clothes — and once it has entered the event stream no consumer can distinguish it from a legitimate delta.

This change enforces the invariant at the producer boundary. When `models.providers.<id>.models[].compat.dropCumulativeTextDeltaReplays` is enabled for a model, the guard classifies each maximal visible-text run of an incoming frame (delta-, message-, or structured-array-shaped) — the run boundaries fall where structured reasoning content interrupts the visible text — and drops a run whose filtered visible text is exactly equal to all the visible text the producer has accepted within the current text block (8+ chars, with a length pre-check so the hot path is untouched) before it reaches the additive accumulation path. The run streams through the reasoning-tag partitioner and the DSML recovery/filter stages into an ordered emit plan, and the guard classifies the plan's total only after every stage has released its buffers, so a run that continues (an incomplete trailing tag, a partial DSML token) never matches early. A run that restates the accepted text exactly carries no characters the consumer has not already accumulated, so suppressing it removes no information while keeping the live stream clean; every run that adds anything flows untouched. The ledger itself advances as filtered visible text is released, before the post-tool-call queue can hold it back. Both visible-text feeders — content deltas and visible reasoning details — route through the same admission seam. Everything else keeps flowing: short exact repeats, long repeats that do not restate the whole block, checkpoint-style replay sequences, and retry streams (which start a fresh stream with a new `text_start` checkpoint) all behave exactly as before.

The flag is wired end to end: it is part of the OpenAI completions compat config whitelist and zod schema, and a schema regression test exercises the config-loaded path. Agent-side accumulation stays a single additive path; the sibling accumulation sites are intentionally unchanged because they are contract-conforming consumers — the violation originates at the producer, which is also where the accumulated text is available to detect it.

On the open question whether OpenClaw should expose this per-model equality heuristic at all: the alternatives considered were a default-on guard and producer snapshot normalization. Default-on changes behavior for every provider, and snapshot normalization is the larger design discussed in #136322 with its own compatibility traps; this PR intentionally takes the narrowest slice that fixes the field-reported defect while leaving default behavior untouched. The discriminator this guard relies on is narrow and observable: the reported replay is a single frame, mid-stream, with no new `text_start`, whose payload exactly equals the accumulated text — unlike checkpoint replays (multi-frame sequences restarting from a boundary) and retry streams (new stream, new checkpoint). If maintainers prefer a different shape (for example a different flag placement or requiring the flag to be set by tooling), happy to adjust.

## User Impact

- Default: no behavior change. The guard is off unless a model's `compat.dropCumulativeTextDeltaReplays` is set to `true` in config. The config surface is purely additive: the key is optional, defaults to false, needs no migration, and configs without it validate exactly as before (covered by the schema regression test).
- Users hitting the doubling bug on affected providers can enable the flag on that model and get stable streaming output without local workarounds. Enabling is per-model and reversible; disabling restores the exact historical append behavior.
- Tradeoff when enabled: content equality cannot distinguish an intentional long repetition (a model deliberately restating its whole answer so far) from a malformed cumulative frame, so such a restatement is suppressed once. When structured reasoning content splits a frame's visible text into runs, each run is judged independently: a run that restates the whole block is suppressed even when a later run adds new text, because the suppressed run itself contributes no new characters. The 8-character floor keeps short natural repeats (`"Ha"`, `"Ha"`) flowing, and partial restatements that add new text are never touched.
- Scope: the guard covers the `openai-completions` producer's visible-text path — the route of the field report and wire capture in #136262. The native DeepSeek plugin route's reasoning-text rendering duplication reported in the issue thread is a separate transport with its own producer boundary (thinking deltas) and is intentionally not addressed here.
- No changes to other providers, other transports, or agent-side accumulation.

## Configuration

Enable per model; the setting is reversible and requires no migration:

```json
{
  "models": {
    "providers": {
      "<provider-id>": {
        "models": [
          {
            "id": "<model-id>",
            "compat": {
              "dropCumulativeTextDeltaReplays": true
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Supported routes: the `openai-completions` transport in both direct and managed modes, covering its visible-text path — plain content deltas, structured content arrays, message-shaped frames, and visible reasoning details. The native DeepSeek plugin transport has its own producer boundary and is out of scope. Disabling the flag restores the exact historical append behavior.

Because the flag is a `compat` override, the standard route-ownership rules apply: bundled and catalog-known model routes resolve compat from the provider catalog, so an authored `compat` block on such a route does not take effect, and `openclaw doctor --fix` reports matching overrides for cleanup. Enablement is therefore effective on genuinely custom providers, custom models, or a catalog model routed to a different endpoint; extending it to a bundled route would be a catalog change. The option defaults to `false` and needs no migration, so existing configurations keep the current additive behavior.

## Evidence

- Focused tests (`packages/ai/src/transports/openai-completions-stream.replay.test.ts`) cover both the direct and managed transports:
  - a bare frame restating the whole accumulated text is dropped, and subsequent deltas keep appending;
  - the historical append behavior is preserved when the flag is off;
  - short exact repeats still append while enabled;
  - long repeats that do not restate the whole message still append while enabled;
  - a message-shaped frame equal to the accumulated text is dropped while enabled and appended while disabled;
  - a cumulative replay arriving after a native `tool_calls` chunk is dropped on both transports, and a legitimate post-tool-call delta restating the earlier block is kept;
  - a cumulative replay after a visible reasoning detail is dropped (reasoning-detail text feeds the same ledger);
  - a cumulative replay of the filtered visible text after inline reasoning tags is dropped (the ledger tracks the visible domain, not raw frames);
  - a cumulative replay after a queued post-tool-call reasoning detail is dropped (admission covers the queued path);
  - a multi-piece restatement frame (reasoning tags between the repeated prefix and further visible text) is dropped whole, and a frame whose repeated prefix is followed by genuine text is kept (the comparison unit is the whole frame's filtered visible contribution);
  - structured content array parts are classified as one frame: a repeated first part followed by a genuine second part is kept;
  - a frame split by structured reasoning content into a full-restatement run followed by a genuine new-text run keeps the new text while the restated run is suppressed, so the accumulated result matches the provider-intended text;
  - a frame ending in an incomplete tag keeps its released prefix (parser buffering), and a frame ending in a partial DSML tool token keeps its released prefix (recovery-stage buffering);
  - a replay hidden behind DSML tool markup is suppressed when the markup filters away, and the same wrapper followed by a genuine suffix is kept (classification runs on the filtered output).
- Released text followed by structured reasoning while tag syntax stays pending keeps its event order (text, then thinking, then text) with the option enabled and disabled, and both produce identical output (`openai-completions-stream.reasoning-and-buffering.test.ts`).
- The split-frame case is also asserted at the delivery-phase level: the interrupted run is tagged `commentary` and the genuine follow-up `final_answer`, so with the flag off and on the `final_answer` block is byte-identical and only the `commentary` block differs (it holds one copy of the repeated run instead of two);
- The config-loaded path is covered by a schema regression test: a provider model with `compat.dropCumulativeTextDeltaReplays: true` validates and preserves the flag (`src/config/config.schema-regressions.test.ts`).
- All `openai-completions` transport suites pass unchanged.
- Negative controls: each review round's regression cases were written before the fix and failed on the then-current head in exactly the way the reviewer traced (per-piece comparison dropping a repeated prefix, and the DSML-wrapped replay slipping through); the suite now covers 41 cases across the direct/managed matrix.
- Event trace through the real transport with the reported frame shape (synthetic SSE frames: three ordinary deltas of 35/30/26 chars, then a frame restating everything so far):

  Guard off (historical behavior — the replay frame is emitted, and additive consumers double the text):

  ```text
  seq=3 text_delta deltaLen=35 accumulatedBefore=0
  seq=4 text_delta deltaLen=30 accumulatedBefore=35
  seq=5 text_delta deltaLen=65 accumulatedBefore=65   <-- full-text replay frame
  seq=6 text_delta deltaLen=26 accumulatedBefore=130
  final emitted additive text length = 156 (clean length is 91)
  ```

  Guard on (same provider frames, flag enabled):

  ```text
  seq=3 text_delta deltaLen=35 accumulatedBefore=0
  seq=4 text_delta deltaLen=30 accumulatedBefore=35
  seq=5 text_delta deltaLen=26 accumulatedBefore=65
  final emitted additive text length = 91
  ```

- How the field symptom connects to incoming provider chunks: the report in #136262 captured the offending frame in the internal assistant event stream (`OPENCLAW_RAW_STREAM=1`), downstream of the transport. To bridge that gap, the reported frame shape — a single mid-stream `choice.delta` whose payload equals the whole accumulated text, with no new `text_start` — was driven through an unmodified v2026.9.3 build (provider transport and agent core) against a controlled provider endpoint. The frame passed through every layer unfiltered, and the live accumulated text ended at 470 characters versus 309 for the identical stream without that frame (the text accumulated up to the replay point is counted twice). The stock transport neither synthesizes nor filters this frame shape, so the field-captured event must have arrived from the provider side of the transport boundary; the one internal alternative, an SDK-level retry resending the full text, was ruled out separately — retry streams restart with a fresh `text_start` checkpoint, which the reported replay lacks. The trace above reproduces this shape end to end.
- A passive wire capture on the affected provider route (17 requests; provider and model identifiers withheld, frame shapes only) saw exclusively delta-shaped text frames — no message snapshots — and no replay within that window; the quirk is intermittent. Nothing observed on the wire marks a frame as a replay, and the field signature reaches the guard as an ordinary bare `text_delta`, so when a replay occurs it differs from a legitimate delta only by its content. Content equality at the producer is the only available trigger, which is why the guard is opt-in and length-floored rather than metadata-based.


